public interface MachineState {
    default void selectItem(VendingMachine m, String code)  { throw invalid("selectItem"); }
    default void insertCoin(VendingMachine m, Coin coin)    { throw invalid("insertCoin"); }
    default void confirmPurchase(VendingMachine m)          { throw invalid("confirmPurchase"); }
    default void cancel(VendingMachine m)                   { throw invalid("cancel"); }
    default InvalidActionException invalid(String op) {
        return new InvalidActionException(op + " not allowed in " + getClass().getSimpleName());
    }
}

public class IdleState implements MachineState {
    @Override
    public void selectItem(VendingMachine m, String code) {
        if (!m.inventory().isInStock(code)) throw new OutOfStockException(code);
        m.setSelectedItem(m.inventory().get(code).item());
        m.setState(new ItemSelectedState());
    }
}

public class CoinInsertedState implements MachineState {
    @Override
    public void insertCoin(VendingMachine m, Coin coin) {
        m.acceptCoin(coin);    // adds to insertedCoins, increments register provisionally
    }
    @Override
    public void confirmPurchase(VendingMachine m) {
        Money price = m.pricing().priceOf(m.selectedItem());
        Money inserted = Coin.sum(m.insertedCoins());
        if (inserted.lessThan(price)) throw new InsufficientFundsException();
        Money changeAmount = inserted.minus(price);

        Optional<List<Coin>> change = m.register().makeChange(changeAmount);
        if (change.isEmpty()) {
            // can't make exact change — refuse and refund
            m.refundInsertedCoins();
            m.setState(new IdleState());
            throw new ExactChangeUnavailableException();
        }
        m.setState(new DispensingState());
        m.dispenseAndReturnChange(change.get());
        m.setState(new IdleState());
    }
    @Override
    public void cancel(VendingMachine m) {
        m.refundInsertedCoins();
        m.setState(new IdleState());
    }
}
```

The `VendingMachine` itself is thin:

```java
public class VendingMachine {
    private MachineState state = new IdleState();
    // inventory, register, pricing, selectedItem, insertedCoins, plus a single mutex

    public synchronized void selectItem(String code)    { state.selectItem(this, code); }
    public synchronized void insertCoin(Coin c)         { state.insertCoin(this, c); }
    public synchronized void confirmPurchase()          { state.confirmPurchase(this); }
    public synchronized void cancel()                   { state.cancel(this); }
}
```

This keeps each state's logic local to its class — no giant switch.

Greedy denomination works for coin systems with the canonical denominations (5, 10, 25, 50, 100 — yes for these; not for arbitrary denominations).

```java
public Optional<List<Coin>> makeChange(int cents) {
    List<Coin> result = new ArrayList<>();
    Map<Denomination, Integer> available = new EnumMap<>(counts);   // copy
    for (Denomination d : Denomination.descending()) {
        while (cents >= d.value() && available.get(d) > 0) {
            result.add(new Coin(d));
            available.put(d, available.get(d) - 1);
            cents -= d.value();
        }
    }
    if (cents != 0) return Optional.empty();   // can't make exact change
    // commit
    counts.putAll(available);
    return Optional.of(result);
}
```

Key points:
- **Try on a copy first;** commit only on success. Half-completed change leaves the register inconsistent.
- **Empty result → refuse the purchase** with `ExactChangeUnavailableException` and refund.
- **For unusual denominations**, greedy can fail when DP would succeed; mention as an extension if interviewer presses.
- **Refunds use the same algorithm** with the user's just-inserted coins as a hint to give back the same denominations preferentially.
